          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A prominent idea in the effort to isolate what are the properly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logical
            concepts we deploy has been to use the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
            of the corresponding domain of objects of the universe of discourse. This approach has been seen by some, notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friederich+Mautner"} Friederich Mautner
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Tarski"} Alfred Tarski
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"} Erlangen program
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Klein"} Felix Klein
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "action#ActionsOfAGroup"} transformation groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean
            concepts are preserved by Euclidean transformations of space, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            ones are preserved by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphisms"} homeomorphisms
          %p
            Tarski’s informal definition (
            %a{:href => "#Ln"}> Ln
            ) is reproduced here,
          %blockquote
            %p Now suppose we continue this idea, and consider still wider classes of transformations. In the extreme case, we would consider the class of all one-one transformations of the space, or universe of discourse, or ‘world’, onto itself. What will be the science which deals with the notions invariant under this widest class of transformations? Here we will have very few notions, all of a very general character. I suggest that they are the logical notions, that we call a notion ‘logical’ if it is invariant under all possible one-one transformations of the world onto itself.
          %p Logic is now to be seen as the maximally invariant theory.
          %p
            Tarski goes on to point out that using this method to distinguish between logical and mathematical concepts, the result is dependent on the chosen foundational theory. When
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"} Russell's
            theory of types is chosen, as used by Whitehead and him in
            = succeed "," do
              %em Principia Mathematica
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %h2#cases_of_invariance_in_logical_systems Cases of invariance in logical systems
          %h3#propositional_logic Propositional logic
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional
            case, consider the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} acting
            on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} cartesian powers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^k
            are
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbits"} orbits
            of its action. The idea then is that such relations are invariant if and only if they are definable using only logical constants.
          %h3#simple_type_theory Simple type theory
          %p
            Johan van Benthem proposed (
            %a{:href => "#JvB"}> JvB
            ) such a treatment for a certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simple type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((e, t), t)
            include the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
          %p Van Benthem demonstrates that types possessing an invariant element are precisely those of either of the forms
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1, (a_2, \ldots,(a_n, t)\ldots)),
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1, (a_2, \ldots,(a_n, e)\ldots)),
                where at least one of the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
                fails to have an invariant element.
          %h3#homotopy_type_theory Homotopy type theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"} Steve Awodey
            (
            %a{:href => "#ULL"}> ULL
            ) has argued that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            should be seen in a similar light as expressing invariance under the broader notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            (see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#UPL"} UPL
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Tarski-Grothendieck Thesis
          %blockquote
            %p If a statement, concept, or construction is purely logical, then it should be invariant under all equivalences of the structures involved. A statement that is not invariant must involve some non-logical specifics, pertaining not to general logical form but to some particular aspects of the objects bearing the structure. If it is the hallmark of a logical concept that it should pertain only to general, formal structure and not to any specific features of the objects bearing that structure, then this formal character may be witnessed by the fact that the concept is invariant under all equivalence transformations.
          %p
            One way to understand this formally is in terms of type formation that is invariant under type equivalence. So given a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: \mathcal{U}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+type"} dependent sum type
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
                formation in
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi BAut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X: BAut(A)) \to (X \to \mathcal{U}) \to \mathcal{U},
            %li
              %p
                list type formation and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncation+modality"} truncation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \| X\|_n
                in
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi BAut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X: BAut(A)) \to \mathcal{U},
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
                formation in
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi BAut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X: BAut(A)) \to X \to (X \to \mathcal{U}),
          %p and so on.
          %p
            Types formed from two or more types may be treated similarly, so that the formation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+type"} sum type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            are invariant elements of
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi BAut
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi BAut
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X: BAut(A)) \to (Y: BAut(B)) \to \mathcal{U}.
          %p
            In this way, any concept expressible in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
